-- Typeclass specs — record-type companions to the registered Typeclass
-- declarations. Phase 10a sub-step 2 of ts-elimination (see
-- workstreams/ts-elimination/briefs/phase-10a-sub-step-2-typeclass-entity-to-record-type.md
-- and design/unified-dispatch.md for the architectural rationale).
--
-- Each Typeclass collapses into a parametric record type:
--   typeclass Labelable a where { label : LocaleString }
-- ↓
--   Labelable a = Labelable label: LocaleString
--
-- A spec record's fields mirror the typeclass's methods (nullary methods
-- project to `field: returns`; multi-param methods project to an `$arrow`
-- field). The spec record type is registered as an ordinary entity in
-- `systemEntities`, so the catalog surfaces it under the new "spec"
-- section, and EntityIdPicker offers `Id<Labelable>`-typed slots the
-- same way it offers any other entity reference.
--
-- Why a Fenel source file (this one) AND a programmatic loop (in
-- system-entities.ts)? The Fenel file is the canonical authoring surface
-- for hand-tuned specs — fields with non-default labels, descriptions, or
-- per-spec overrides land here so the .fenel source records the intent.
-- The programmatic loop in system-entities.ts emits a spec for every
-- Typeclass that doesn't already have a hand-authored counterpart here
-- (precedence: hand-authored wins; programmatic fills the long tail).
-- Together they ensure every Typeclass has a spec record entity, while
-- the curated ones stay readable in Fenel syntax.
--
-- Field name convention: each field's name matches the typeclass method
-- name (`label`, `codeName`, `description`, `icon`, …). Field type is the
-- method's declared `returns` type (or `$arrow` for non-nullary methods).
-- The constructor's tag is `<ClassName>Spec` (e.g. `Labelable`).
--
-- The hand-authored set below covers the representational core
-- (Labelable / Codeable / Describable / Iconable / Emojiable /
-- Categorizable / Taggable / Viewable / Aliasable / Inputable /
-- Exemplifiable). Phase 10b's representational-export work iterates over
-- these — keeping them in Fenel source means the parser round-trip
-- verifies their shape on every engine boot.

-- Phase 10a sub-step 3 — Verified envelope + Evidence ADT. Spec record
-- fields holding laws use `Verified Boolean` for closed-form predicates
-- over the spec's methods. The construction-time validator
-- (law-validation.ts:validateVerified) dispatches on Evidence to grade
-- the kind of justification a Verified value carries. Forward-compatible
-- with full theorem proving: `KernelProven` carries a Fenel-expression
-- reference (a fnId today; future kernel extension will reduction-check
-- the function's body against the predicate). See
-- design/laws-and-proofs.md Stage 10+ and the no-Proof-type principle in
-- workstreams/ts-elimination/briefs/phase-10a-typeclass-collapse-design.md.
--
-- expr-substrate Phase 6b (Q1 arm A — Rom-locked 2026-06-11) — `External
-- source: String` carries the PROVENANCE of an un-re-checkable external
-- attestation (an LLM / human / third-party tool that asserted the law but
-- whose proof the kernel cannot re-check; an external source whose proof term
-- the kernel CAN re-check just calls `kernel:verify` and earns plain
-- SolverChecked / Proven). The Evidence lattice IS the strategy lattice —
-- NO separate `strategy` field ships (it would be a third near-identical
-- lattice alongside Evidence / EpistemicStatus, the duplicate-pattern smell
-- AGENTS.md says to collapse). `validateVerified` grades External as Assumed
-- (A-minimal: the provenance lives in the evidence value; the status needn't
-- repeat it). See design/laws-and-proofs.md "External sources".

Evidence = Axiom | SampleTested count: Nat | SolverChecked | KernelProven proof: String | External source: String

Verified p = Verified predicate: p evidence: Evidence

-- Phase 10a sub-step 4 — DispatchKey: typed dispatch key for spec registrations.
-- Three variants spanning the dispatch lattice (see Phase 10a design pass §6
-- and workstreams/ts-elimination/briefs/phase-10a-sub-step-4-dispatch-keys-typed-ids.md):
--   - ByType: dispatch on a typed Id<Type> — today's path, retains nominal-
--             typeclassing semantics. The target field carries the spec
--             codename (e.g. "Post"), a string like Evidence's KernelProven
--             proof, for unresolved-but-tolerated forward refs. Future migration to a proper Id<Type>
--             slots in via the same channel once Id<Type> is a registered
--             system entity (Stage 12+ of structural-introspection).
--   - ByValue: dispatch on a literal value — for value-keyed registrations
--              (e.g. dispatch on "the bot named 'main'"). The value field
--              carries the raw JSON-Logic value matched against the
--              dispatched-on argument at lookup time.
--   - ByPattern: dispatch on a structural predicate over types — for shape-
--                based codecs / renderers / solvers. The predicate field is
--                a fnId resolving to a `Type -> Bool` function. RESERVED —
--                today's runtime collects ByPattern registrations but doesn't
--                yet match them at lookup time (Stage 10+ of structural-
--                dispatch; needs Prover dispatch over predicate narrowing).

DispatchKey = ByType target: String | ByValue value: Any | ByPattern predicate: String

-- LocaleString — Fenel-side reference to the locale-tagged string type
-- defined in TS (locale.ts). Today: a JS `string | { [locale]: string }`
-- union; modeled here as a $named ref so spec fields can carry it. A
-- future migration may surface LocaleString as a proper Fenel ADT
-- (Mono String | PerLocale (Map String String)); the spec records adapt
-- via codeName-keyed lookup once that lands.

-- Identifiable surface — every named Fenel thing has one of these
-- speccing its human-facing identity. The `*NonEmpty` fields hold the
-- canonical representational laws (e.g. labels / icons must be non-empty
-- strings); they're `Verified Boolean` because the underlying predicates
-- are closed-form Bool checks.
--
-- `Codeable` carries NO `codeNameNonEmpty` law (dropped — typed-function-
-- references, Rom 2026-06-05): non-emptiness of a code-name is a *refinement*
-- of the field's type, not a theorem worth a per-field `Verified` proof
-- obligation (the heavy law machinery is for invariants a refined type can't
-- state — cross-field / semantic). `Describable` already carries none, so
-- `Codeable` now mirrors it. The boot encoding never populated the proof
-- anyway, so every Codeable instance was a standing (unenforced) law
-- violation; dropping it removes that noise. A future refinement may retype
-- `codeName : NonEmptyLocaleString` to carry the invariant intrinsically.

Labelable a = Labelable label: LocaleString labelNonEmpty: Verified Boolean

Codeable a = Codeable codeName: LocaleString

Describable a = Describable description: LocaleString

-- Submittable — the public submit-CTA representation (storage-as-effect Phase
-- 4e, Rom 2026-06-19). A "form" is the auto-derived input view over a type; its
-- submit-button label is one of "the type's representations", not a datum on a
-- separate form object. `submitLabel` is the CTA text (e.g. "APPLY FOR FREE"),
-- resolved by type id like every other type-level representation. Declared
-- LAW-FREE (no `Verified` field), like `Describable` / `HasDefault` ⇒ no audit-law
-- count change. Persists the LocaleString as a bare canonical string (mirroring
-- Codeable's codeName), so the read needs no canonical() unwrap. This dissolves
-- the last non-superseded datum of the retired `entity_type='form'` rows
-- (config.submit_label). See design/forms-collapse.md + the Phase-4e brief.
Submittable a = Submittable submitLabel: LocaleString

Iconable a = Iconable icon: String iconNonEmpty: Verified Boolean

-- Symbolable — the GENERAL math-symbol projection (containment-substrate Phase 2
-- Step 3, design §10.3). A type/former's display GLYPH (`∪` for union, `¬` for
-- complement, `→` for arrow, …) is a per-target static representation resolved by
-- NAME, exactly like `Iconable`'s `icon` or `Labelable`'s `label`
-- ([[project_value_parametric_representations_deferred]]). It retires the
-- formatter-hardcoded glyph strings (`set-syntax.fenel:formatSetExpr` +
-- `ExpressionDisplay.tsx`) into the representation substrate — the hardcoded glyph
-- IS the [[feedback_no_language_concept_in_ts]] violation; this is its `.fenel`
-- home. The per-former glyph instances live in `fenel/std/dispatch-instances.fenel`
-- (union/intersect/complement/diff/top/bottom/arrow).
--
-- `Symbolable = Infixable + 0` is the design-lock §5 framing: `Infixable.notation`
-- IS `Symbolable`'s infix-position case (`Infixable = Symbolable + fixity`). The
-- `Infixable`/`Prefixable` fixity classes are NOT merged away here — they keep
-- `precedence`/`associativity`; sourcing their `notation` from this projection is
-- the representation-substrate FOLLOW-ON (S4, split per the brief's scope rider).
--
-- Declared LAW-FREE (no `Verified` field), like `Describable` / `HasDefault` ⇒ no
-- audit-law count change. Pure-signature (one `symbol: String` method, no super /
-- law / body-default), so it derives in `pureClasses` (system-entities.ts) like
-- the other pure representational classes. NOT marked `Representational` this step:
-- the instances are keyed by FORMER NAME (`"union"`), not by a registered entity
-- type, so surfacing a per-type symbol editor in RepresentationsPanel would be
-- empty for every type — the value-parametric / per-type Symbolable membership
-- rides the representation-substrate follow-on alongside S4.
Symbolable a = Symbolable symbol: String

-- Subsumable — the instance-extensible numeric-subtype relation (containment-
-- substrate Phase 3 Step 2). The named-type subtyping facts (ℕ⊂ℤ⊂ℚ⊂ℝ — a
-- *mathematical* fact the structural trio does NOT derive structurally; the
-- honesty rider: *something* must assert `isInt x ⇒ isRational x`) used to live
-- trapped in a TS const (`set-algebra.ts` `NAMED_HIERARCHY`) — the
-- [[feedback_no_language_concept_in_ts]] violation. Here the assertion moves to a
-- `.fenel` instance-extensible relation: adding a numeric type to the hierarchy =
-- add a `Subsumable` instance, NOT edit a TS map. `supers` is the list of named
-- types that this type is a subset of (its known supersets). The `engine:namedSubset`
-- decider (`set-algebra-deciders.fenel`, UNCHANGED) traverses these rows as data;
-- this phase only flips its DATA SOURCE from the TS const to these instances.
-- The structural derivation (deriving `ℤ⊂ℚ` from predicate-implication) is a
-- `solver-extension` / D-3 thread — NOT this class, which is the explicit assertion.
--
-- Pure-signature (one `supers: List String` method, no super / law / body-default,
-- the `Taggable a = Taggable tags: List String` precedent) ⇒ derives in
-- `pureClasses` (system-entities.ts), law-free ⇒ audit-count-neutral. Keyed by the
-- named-type STRING (`Subsumable "Nat"`), the `Symbolable "union"` precedent, so the
-- projection lands on `args: ["Nat"]` matching `listInstances("Subsumable")`.
Subsumable a = Subsumable supers: List String

Emojiable a = Emojiable emoji: String emojiNonEmpty: Verified Boolean

-- A picture is ordinary Image data. The shared glyph planner selects a
-- picture before the same identity's icon or emoji; both remain fallbacks
-- while its picture is unavailable. This facet introduces no host capability.
@label "Picture representation"
@fieldLabel "picture" "Picture"
Picturable a = Picturable picture: Image

-- `sound` stays declared `String` (typed-function-references Phase 3):
-- Soundable has a class decl but 0 instances today, so there is no value to
-- convert and no arrow to pin yet. Tighten when the first instance lands.
Soundable a = Soundable sound: String

-- Categorizable / Taggable — flat-string category + free-form tag list.
-- Distinct from Groupable (named groups) and typeclass membership (typed).

Categorizable a = Categorizable category: String

Taggable a = Taggable tags: List String

-- Viewable — TypeView nomination for the universal renderer. Two slots:
-- `defaultView` for the full read display, `defaultInlineView` for ref
-- badges / dropdowns / compact contexts.

Viewable a = Viewable defaultView: String defaultInlineView: String

-- Aliasable — non-Fenel target-language identifiers (Python, JS, SQL, URL
-- slug, …). The map is keyed by target-language name; today the keys are
-- bare strings, future may type them via a TargetLanguage ADT.

Aliasable a = Aliasable aliases: Map String String

-- Exemplifiable — sample values for the storybook. The `examples` field
-- is parametric over the spec's type variable `a`.

Exemplifiable a = Exemplifiable examples: List a

-- Storyboardable — function-side cousin of Exemplifiable; carries
-- (args, output) example rows for callable storyboards.

Storyboardable f = Storyboardable storyboard: List f

-- Layout — UI dispatch for the dashboard's home view shapes (canvas,
-- list, grid, tree, calendar, timeline, map). The `kind` string keys the
-- renderer to the right layout strategy. Constructor name is
-- `Layout` (not `Layoutable`) because the typeclass itself is
-- named `Layout` in builtin-classes.ts (the variable is `layoutable`).

Layout a = Layout kind: String

-- Inputable — default input-channel list per type. `InputChannel` is a
-- TS-only type today (input-strategies.ts); the spec records its name.

Inputable a = Inputable channels: List InputChannel

-- Referenceable — every entity has an identity-field name. Used by the
-- registry to extract a row's unique id from its data.

Referenceable a = Referenceable refId: String = "id"

-- Groupable — entity types that can be organized into named groups.
-- Subclass of Referenceable: the `referenceable: Referenceable a` field is a
-- SUPERCLASS obligation (a registered class applied to the class param — an
-- `$el` type-level application), NOT a method; the field-type classifier
-- (`effect-dispatch.ts:isSuperclassField`/`supersOf`) erases it and stamps
-- `seed.supers = [Referenceable a]`. `groupStructure` carries a refined string
-- ∈ {"flat", "tree"} but is typed plain String here (the refinement lives in
-- the law, not the field type); its `= "flat"` is the DEFAULT METHOD BODY (the
-- field's default value, Phase 7d). `structureValid` is the canonical
-- structural law (groupStructure ∈ {"flat", "tree"}) as a `Verified Boolean`
-- field — erased from the dispatch method set. Groupable composes all three
-- class-declaration features: super (7b′) + law (7bc) + body default (7d).

Groupable a = Groupable referenceable: Referenceable a groupName: String groupLabel: String groupStructure: String = "flat" structureValid: Verified Boolean

-- HasDefault — types that supply a sensible default value of their
-- target type. The `default` field is parametric over `a`.

HasDefault a = HasDefault default: a

-- Displayable — per-FIELD UI-hint facet. The instance arg is a Field UUID,
-- carrying how to
-- input/display THAT field: `@widget "<field>" "<widget-name>"` +
-- `@helpText "<field>" "<text>"` directives lift into one `Displayable`
-- instance per field. Distinct from `Inputable` (keyed by TYPE — input
-- *channels* per type, "how to input values OF a type"); this is keyed by a
-- Field UUID, a sibling concern. Reuses the existing `field_ui_hint` record
-- (the entity form of `EditDisplay`: widget / helpText / placeholder / prefix /
-- suffix / columns / options / …) as the value — no parallel hint struct.
-- Declared LAW-FREE (no `Verified` field), like `Describable` / `HasDefault`:
-- a widget hint has no meaningful validity theorem, so a law-bearing variant
-- would be permanent unproven boilerplate (each unproven `Verified` field reads
-- as one audit law violation). Count-neutral by construction.

Displayable a = Displayable hint: field_ui_hint

-- Undefinable — types whose values may be absent in non-Fenel
-- representations (JSON Schema's `required: false`, JSON's `undefined`,
-- SQL's NULL). Defaults to false; only Maybe-shaped types should set it.

Undefinable a = Undefinable isErasable: Boolean = False

-- ============================================================================
-- Phase 2 of typeclass-substrate-completion — migrated specs (2026-05-28)
--
-- The 46 specs below were programmatically generated from TS SpecRecordSeed
-- declarations via scripts/generate-phase2-specs.ts and reviewed.
-- They retire Stage 2 of the seed-walk for uppercase-named typeclasses.
-- Lowercase-named capability typeclasses (messaging / payment / ai / email /
-- telegram_*) remain programmatically derived — renaming touches persisted DB
-- data (SpecInstance.typeName); deferred to Phase 2.5.
-- ============================================================================

-- Types whose values can be migrated from a previous registry version's shape to this version.
Versionable a = Versionable fromPrevious: String

-- Types whose values can be sampled at a coordinate to yield a value.
-- `sample` stays declared `String` deliberately (typed-function-references
-- Phase 3): the instance VALUES are now typed `{$ref}` function refs, but the
-- per-modality arrows diverge (Picture: Point -> Color; Sound: Time -> Amplitude;
-- Movie/Animation/Behavior/Event each differ), so there is no single coherent
-- general arrow like Renderable's `Markup -> a`. The runtime dispatches off the
-- instance value (not this field type), so the value conversion is the
-- dispatch-correct change; tightening the decl awaits a coordinate-parametric
-- shape (a `Sampleable c a` / associated-type form), out of scope here.
Sampleable a = Sampleable sample: String

Renderable a = Renderable render: Markup -> a mime_type: String max_length: Maybe<Number>

-- Types whose values can be serialised to a downloadable format (PNG, WAV, SVG, …).
Exportable a = Exportable formats: [String]

-- Functions that dispatch to a runtime IO handler.
IOInvokable a = IOInvokable ioAction: String stopAction: Maybe<String> listInstancesAction: Maybe<String>

-- Marker typeclass. An instance `Representational :: X` declares that the typeclass `X` is a way of representing a value (label, icon, description, …) and should surface in the shared RepresentationsPanel UX.
Representational c = Representational

-- Field-specific representation family. Unlike Representational, this marker
-- is safe for the field entity editor and includes Displayable without exposing
-- field_ui_hint on unrelated function/type/value panels.
FieldRepresentational c = FieldRepresentational

-- Marker for capabilities that touch the network — any protocol.
HasNetwork scope = HasNetwork

-- HTTP / HTTPS capability. Subclass of HasNetwork. The `network: HasNetwork scope`
-- field is a SUPERCLASS obligation, NOT a method: its type is a registered class
-- applied to the class param (an `$el` type-level application = dependent typing),
-- the SAME erasable-obligation shape a `Verified P` law field has. The field-type
-- classifier (effect-dispatch.ts:isSuperclassField) erases it from the dispatch
-- method set and stamps it as a super; the witness is resolved by reflection at
-- use, never threaded as a stored slot (Phase 7b′; design/unified-dispatch.md §140-148).
HasHTTP scope = HasHTTP network: HasNetwork scope request: { } get: { } post: { }

-- Durable state — create / read / update / list instances, append events, and
-- the type/view operations carried by the account runtime handler dictionary.
-- Landing 1 A1/A2 makes the declaration's method roster total for that authored
-- dictionary: resolution must retain all nine descriptor fields rather than
-- silently projecting the four previously-undeclared methods away.
HasStateIO scope = HasStateIO createInstance: String getInstance: Maybe<{ }> updateInstance: Unit listInstances: [{ }] appendEvent: Unit loadType: Maybe<{ }> schemaOf: Type tryCreateInstanceByName: { ok: Boolean, value: Maybe (EntityRef<"instance">), error: Maybe (String) } pinToView: Unit

-- storage-as-effect Phase 1b (2026-06-19) — the BACKEND behind `HasStateIO` as a
-- scoped-instance dispatch (design/storage-as-effect.md D1 `(c)∘(b)`). Storage
-- location is NOT a new effect kind — it is a HANDLER SELECTION under the existing
-- `HasStateIO`. `StorageHandler` is a typeclass over a phantom-tagged `backend`
-- (`StorageHandler Db` = the existing `@fenel/db` leaves relocated; `StorageHandler
-- Memory` = a fresh in-process backend; `File` = a documented future tag). Its
-- methods are the storage leaf set: store / load / update / delete / list /
-- appendEvent / loadType / loadTypeByName. `HasStateIO`'s create/read/update/
-- delete/list/appendEvent handlers resolve the ACTIVE `StorageHandler` instance
-- (base scope = `Memory`; DB-configured boot installs `Db`) and dispatch its
-- method, instead of hard-calling `@fenel/db`.
--
-- Each method holds a reference to one of the @native declarations in
-- fenel/modules/storage/types.fenel (`store = storage.dbStore`) and is typed as
-- that leaf is, its placeholder returns included (that module's header says why
-- a declared `StoredRow` is deferred); a method takes its arguments together,
-- the arity of the leaves. The irreducible DB / in-memory / bundled-register host
-- leaves live behind that module's ffi boundary; no StorageHandler value carries
-- a host callable, and a reference is read only against the storage module's own
-- declarations. `backend` is the phantom tag the scope dispatches on; it appears
-- in no argument, so the methods are selected by instance lookup.
StorageHandler backend = StorageHandler store: (String, String, Map String Any, String) -> { id: String } load: (String, String) -> Maybe ({ }) update: (String, String, Map String Any) -> Maybe ({ }) delete: (String, String) -> Unit list: (String, String, Maybe (Number), Maybe (Map String Any)) -> [{ }] appendEvent: { accountId: String, type: String, payload: Maybe ({ }), aggregateType: Maybe (String), aggregateId: Maybe (String), profileId: Maybe (String) } -> Unit loadType: (String, String) -> Maybe ({ }) loadTypeByName: (String, String) -> Maybe ({ })

-- Standard input — read lines / bytes from a terminal-like source.
HasStdin scope = HasStdin readLine: String readBytes: Bytes

-- Standard output — write lines / bytes.
HasStdout scope = HasStdout writeLine: Unit writeBytes: Unit

-- Local file system — read / write / list.
HasFileSystem scope = HasFileSystem readFile: Bytes writeFile: Unit listDir: [String]

-- Clock and scheduling — current time, sleep, schedule callbacks.
HasTime scope = HasTime now: Int sleep: Unit schedule: Unit

-- Pseudo-random — generate ints, bytes, UUIDs.
HasRandom scope = HasRandom nextInt: Int nextBytes: Bytes uuid: String

-- Email — send / receive. Subclass of HasNetwork (the `network` super field — see HasHTTP).
HasEmail scope = HasEmail network: HasNetwork scope send: Unit receive: Maybe<{ }>

-- Social platform — post, fetch, list followers. Per-platform handlers via scope; per-impl variants (API vs scraper) via further scoping. Subclass of HasHTTP (the `http` super field — see HasHTTP).
HasSocialMedia scope = HasSocialMedia http: HasHTTP scope sendPost: Unit getPost: Maybe<{ }> listFollowers: [{ }]

-- Logging — emit info / warn / error lines.
HasLog scope = HasLog log: Unit warn: Unit error: Unit

-- Telegram Bot API — run / stop a collector, handle incoming updates, look up bots, send messages. Subclass of HasHTTP (the `http` super field — see HasHTTP).
HasTelegramBot scope = HasTelegramBot http: HasHTTP scope runCollector: Id stopCollector: Unit handleUpdate: Unit findBotById: Maybe<{ }> sendMessageText: Unit sendFieldPrompt: Unit

-- Per-compilation Wasm module-builder capability. Methods intern strings / imports / function types, allocate locals, accumulate pending closure-body functions, track the closure-id counter — the mutable state that the TS lowerToWasmAst threads through its helpers, exposed to a Fenel-bodied port via algebraic-effect dispatch.
HasWasmBuilder scope = HasWasmBuilder internString: Number internImport: Number internImportInModule: Number internType: Number allocLocal: Number setLocalAlias: Unit lookupLocal: Maybe<{ }> snapshotLocals: { } restoreLocals: Unit addPendingFunc: Unit addPendingExport: Unit nextClosureId: Number getEntryName: String

-- Registry reflection — read / mutate the in-process dispatch + entity registry
-- (list system entities, module rosters, instance lookup, register/unregister).
-- effect-visibility Phase 2a: the class the `dispatch:*` IO leaves declare. NOT
-- HasStateIO — the registry is process-memory reflection over the loaded build,
-- not durable storage (a leaf that ALSO touches storage, e.g.
-- refreshAccountInstances, declares both).
HasRegistry scope = HasRegistry listSystemEntities: [{ }] listModuleEntities: [{ }] listModuleFunctions: [{ }] getModuleFunction: Maybe<{ }> lookup: { } lookupAll: [{ }] lookupOne: Maybe<{ }> register: Unit unregister: Unit

-- Generic IO / suspension — read, sleep, wait, emit, end. Language-level effects not tied to a specific external resource.
HasIO scope = HasIO read: Unit sleep: Unit wait: Unit end_function: Unit emit: Unit call_function: { }

-- Structured concurrency + error-handling primitives — bracket, retry, timeout, on_error, parallel, race, any, for_each.
HasControlFlow scope = HasControlFlow bracket: { } retry: { } with_timeout: { } on_error: { } parallel: [{ }] race: { } any: { } for_each: Unit

-- Functions that can be rendered and parsed as infix operators. The editor consults Infixable instances to display calls as `a + b` instead of `add(a, b)`; the parser uses them to recognise infix tokens with the right precedence and associativity.
Infixable a = Infixable notation: String precedence: Nat associativity: String

-- Functions that can be rendered as unary prefix operators — e.g. `not x`, `-x`. The editor consults Prefixable instances to display calls with the prefix notation and to surface them alongside infix operators in the picker.
Prefixable a = Prefixable notation: String precedence: Nat

-- Types with a notion of boolean coercion — each type defines its own falsy values. Bool = False; Number = 0; String = empty; List = Nil; Maybe = Nothing.
Truthy a = Truthy truthy: a -> Boolean

-- Tree-hierarchical types with depth-as-typeclass-arg. Each instance carries depth-ordering / path-validation / depth-truncation ops keyed off the typeclass parameter (the level ADT constructor). Phase 4 of field-meta-collapse — supersedes `treeLattice` (resolution.ts:193-222) for `Place : GeoLevel -> Type`.
Resolvable a = Resolvable compareLevel: (a, a) -> Number validateGeoPath: a -> Boolean truncateToLevel: a -> a

-- Per-entity-type default filter for entity-ref pickers. When a `$tagged Id<X>`-typed field doesn't specify its own `ui.refFilter`, the renderer falls back to this instance's `defaultFilter` Expression. Phase 5b.2 of field-meta-collapse — absorbs the duplicated self-exclude expression at the two pre-5b.2 writer sites.
Filterable a = Filterable defaultFilter: Expression

-- Types `a` that can be encoded into a representation `s`. Total but fallible — `encode : a -> Result String s` may produce an EncodeError (a message String today). Pair with Decodable for a (possibly lossy) bidirection; pair with Iso for a bijective one. The field holds the encoding function itself (`encode = compileJs.serializeJsModule`); `a` is not the class's first parameter, so the method stays selected by instance lookup, not by an argument's type.
Encodable s a = Encodable encode: a -> Result String s

-- Representations `s` that can be decoded back into values of type `a`. Partial — invalid encodings yield a DecodeError (a message String today). Compose via Kleisli (Result-chained) to build longer parse pipelines from primitive decoders. The field holds the decoding function itself (`decode = codec.decodeJsonInt`). Two class parameters, so the method is selected by the complete instance type, never by one argument's type (modules-as-values Phase 2d).
Decodable s a = Decodable decode: s -> Result String a

-- Bijective codec between a serialized form `s` (string/bytes/wire) and a structured form `a` (typed Fenel value). Round-trip in both directions (both laws). Compose via Iso ∘ Iso — both directions and the round-trip proof fall out. The fields hold the functions themselves (`encode = codec.base64Encode`). Iso declares no superclass: an `Iso s a` instance does not make `Encodable s a` or `Decodable s a` resolve. Two class parameters, so the methods are selected by the complete instance type (modules-as-values Phase 2d).
Iso s a = Iso encode: a -> Result String s decode: s -> Result String a isoDecodeEncodeRoundtrip: Verified Boolean isoEncodeDecodeRoundtrip: Verified Boolean

-- Lossy bidirectional pair: `view : s -> a` projects (drops information), `put : (s, a) -> s` writes a focus back into a whole, which carries what `view` dropped. Generalises Iso to cases where the reverse direction needs extra information. The fields hold the functions themselves (`view = codec.recordFirstNameView`); `put` takes its two arguments together, the arity of the functions that implement it. The class parameter `ctx` appears in no argument (for a record-field lens `ctx = s`), so the methods stay selected by instance lookup. The three classical lens laws (GetPut / PutGet / PutPut) are sample-tested via Phase 4's Prover surface and promoted to SampleTested epistemic status by the kernel.
-- `put` is spelled flat because the validator counts an arrow's written arity, not its flattened one; the curried `put : s -> a -> s` waits for that fix.
Lens ctx s a = Lens view: s -> a put: (s, a) -> s lensGetPut: Verified Boolean lensPutGet: Verified Boolean lensPutPut: Verified Boolean

-- Structural set shapes whose membership / subset / disjointness / emptiness are decidable — and (decide-substrate Phase 1) whose witness SYNTHESIS + finite ENUMERATION are part of the same theory surface: `witness` is the constructive dual of `decideEmpty` (a Just w proves non-emptiness by exhibition), `enumerate` the complete-finite-extension reader (`Just [] ≠ Nothing`). Each shape (Interval, Enum, Pattern, Union, …) is a DecidableMembership instance keyed by its shape tag `s`, and its fields hold the per-shape procedures themselves (`contains = set.setIntervalContains`). Every procedure takes any SetExpr; a shape-specific one given a set of another shape answers `Nothing`. `s` appears in no argument, so the methods are selected by instance lookup, not by an argument's type. The set algebra (set-algebra.ts) calls the Fenel deciders directly, not these instances.
DecidableMembership s = DecidableMembership contains: (SetExpr, Any) -> Maybe Boolean decideSubset: (SetExpr, SetExpr) -> Maybe Boolean decideDisjoint: (SetExpr, SetExpr) -> Maybe Boolean decideEmpty: SetExpr -> Maybe Boolean witness: SetExpr -> Maybe Any enumerate: SetExpr -> Maybe (List Any) decidableMembershipSubsetSoundness: Verified Boolean decidableMembershipDisjointSoundness: Verified Boolean decidableMembershipEmptySoundness: Verified Boolean decidableMembershipWitnessSoundness: Verified Boolean

-- Proof strategies. Each instance is one strategy (`Structural`, `Smt`) and holds its proposer, `propose : Any -> Result UnprovedReason Any`. Proposers are untrusted: the kernel (engine/src/kernel.ts) re-checks every term they produce from scratch. The proof-term shape is what earns kernel acceptance, not the proposer's identity — AI, SMT, manual tactics all sit behind the same Prover dispatch surface. See `design/structural-introspection.md` Instance 3.
Prover strategy = Prover propose: Any -> Result UnprovedReason Any

-- Compilation targets for Fenel programs. Each instance is one target, keyed by what its compiler returns (JsModule / WasmModule / LlvmModule / …); `compile : CompileSource -> Result CompileError target` lowers a Fenel source through typed-IR dialects to that target's representation. The field holds the compiler function itself (`compile = compileJs.compileFenelToJs`). Composition with an `Encodable` instance produces the wire form. See `design/structural-introspection.md` Instance 6.
Compilable target = Compilable compile: CompileSource -> Result CompileError target compilableSemanticEquivalence: Verified Boolean

-- Evaluation strategies for Fenel expressions. Each instance is one strategy (EagerStrategy / LazyStrategy / PartialStrategy / JitStrategy / future TracedStrategy / …); `eval : (Expression, EvaluateContext) -> Result EvalError Value` interprets a JSON-Logic AST under that strategy. The field holds the strategy function itself (`eval = evaluatorStrategies.eagerEval`). Per-scope dispatch (block / tag / global) lets a single `.fenel` file introduce a different evaluator for a sub-computation. PartialEvaluator returns a residual expression in the value channel; composing it with `Compilable.compile` derives the Futamura projection — a compiler from the interpreter, no bespoke JIT infrastructure. See `design/structural-introspection.md` Instance 7.
Evaluator strategy = Evaluator eval: (Expression, EvaluateContext) -> Result EvalError Value partialEvaluatorSoundness: Verified Boolean

-- Aggregator functions whose structural properties (input/output types, monotonicity, preimage/image SetExpr functions) the set-algebra can read for composition-shape reasoning. When a `$where` predicate contains `{call: [aggFnName, value]} <op> K`, the algebra looks up `Aggregable [aggFnName]` and uses the lifted output-set to decide subset / disjoint / empty queries — restoring the structural reasoning the retired `$preimage` SetExpr constructor enabled (Phase 3 → Phase 9 closure). `imageOf` / `preimageOf` hold the functions themselves (`imageOf = aggregable.lengthImageOf`), each `SetExpr -> Maybe SetExpr`.
Aggregable agg = Aggregable inputType: FenelType outputType: FenelType monotonicity: Monotonicity imageOf: SetExpr -> Maybe SetExpr preimageOf: SetExpr -> Maybe SetExpr aggregablePreimageRoundtrip: Verified Boolean

-- Phone number validation rules — derived from Country entity data
PhoneFormat country = PhoneFormat dialCode: String pattern: SetExpr minDigits: Nat maxDigits: Nat exampleLocal: String

-- Address structure as a first-class TYPE — each country's postal address is a
-- named `$record` (UsAddress / CaAddress / … in fenel/std/entities/addresses.fenel)
-- the universal FieldRenderer drives. `addressType : FenelType` is the exact
-- type-valued-method shape Aggregable's `inputType` uses; the per-country field
-- labels live on the record's FieldDefs, not as separate methods (Phase 3.3c
-- collapsed the prior 4 methods — addressFields / postalName / stateName /
-- cityName — into this one).
AddressFormat country = AddressFormat addressType: FenelType

-- Social media handle validation — rules differ per platform
HandleFormat platform = HandleFormat pattern: SetExpr prefix: String maxLength: Nat urlTemplate: String

-- Currency exchange rate — derived from Currency entity data, refreshed from external feeds
ExchangeRate currency = ExchangeRate rate: ℝ decimals: ℕ

-- Entity has geographic geometry — coordinates are numbers in angle/deg units. Geometry data is typically virtual (supplied by a FieldProvider binding).
Spatial a = Spatial geometryField: FieldRef geometryType: String centroidField: FieldRef geometryTypeValid: Verified Boolean

-- Date string format — placeholder, separator, and segment order vary by locale
DateFormat locale = DateFormat pattern: SetExpr template: String order: [String] separator: String example: String

-- Storage + HasPreview moved to their module-local owner
-- `fenel/modules/ecom/typeclasses.fenel` (signature-substrate Phase 4-prep,
-- D0b — the ecom `Interface` literals + these std copies consolidated onto
-- one source; the social `interfaces.fenel` module-local precedent).

Social platform = Social read_post: PostId -> Post create_post: PostBody -> Post get_metrics: PostId -> Engagement list_recent: (Username, Int) -> [Post]

Conversational platform = Conversational post_reply: (PostId, String) -> Post get_thread: PostId -> [Post]

Discoverable platform = Discoverable search: Query -> [Post] trending: [Post]


-- Whether a migration's backward direction is well-defined. `Lossless`
-- migrations round-trip exactly (`backward(forward(v1)) = Ok v1`); `Lossy`
-- migrations forget information and carry no backward function. The wire
-- shapes are `{$tag:"Lossless"}` / `{$tag:"Lossy", reason}`
-- (engine/src/migration.ts).
Losslessness = Lossless | Lossy reason: String

-- The failure a migration direction returns. Wire shapes as the migration
-- natives build them (`{$tag:"FieldMissing", field}`, …; engine/src/migration.ts).
-- The invariant case is `InvariantViolation`, not `InternalError`: a constructor
-- name two types declare is ambiguous metadata, and `CompileError` owns that one.
MigrationError = FieldMissing field: String
  | TypeIncompatible field: String expected: String actual: String
  | DefaultUnavailable field: String
  | InvariantViolation message: String

-- Migration — typed schema evolution. The fields hold the functions
-- themselves (`forward = migration.profileDemoV1ToV2`); `backward` is
-- `Just` a function on a Lossless migration and `Nothing` on a Lossy one.
-- `roundtripSamples` are the v1 values that drive the two laws.
Migration v1 v2 = Migration forward: v1 -> Result MigrationError v2 backward: Maybe (v2 -> Result MigrationError v1) losslessness: Losslessness roundtripSamples: List v1 migrationRoundtripLossless: Verified Boolean migrationForwardTotal: Verified Boolean

-- ============================================================================
-- Phase 8a of typed-function-references (2026-06-05) — the OPEN `Identifiable h`
-- handle class, declared INERTLY.
--
-- The north-star (Phase 8): fold the opaque `Id<X>` handle onto the same
-- id-backed mechanism as the transparent `ref`, with resolution becoming
-- DISPATCH over the handle kind. `Identifiable h` is that dispatch class —
-- `resolve` turns a handle of kind `h` into the referenced instance. New handle
-- kinds (UUID today; content-hash / path / version-pinned later) become additive
-- instances with zero bridge edits (the open-class choice, D-A, Rom 2026-06-05).
--
-- "INERTLY": this class is REGISTERED (it derives a dispatch seed in
-- `system-entities.ts` `pureClasses`, surfaces in the catalog) but NOTHING
-- resolves through it yet. A class nobody dispatches on is a no-op → zero
-- behaviour change, zero dispatch-oracle movement. This is the
-- `Infixable`/`Defaultable` playbook — the class exists first, the dispatch flip
-- is a separate, separately-gated change.
--
-- 8c STATUS (2026-06-05): Step 1 LANDED `state:getInstanceByName` (the missing
-- CodeableName resolve target — both handle kinds now name registered fns). The
-- resolution FLIP (wiring `resolve` to the live deref path) is DEFERRED on a
-- dispatch-key blocker the audit surfaced: there is NO generic two-armed `Id<X>`
-- deref bridge to retire (every deref is an explicit `getInstance <typeName>
-- <id>` leaf call, handle kind statically UUID), AND the runtime value-type
-- dispatch keys on `typeConstructorOf(handle)`, which is `"String"` for EVERY
-- handle value — so it cannot select the UUID vs CodeableName instance from the
-- value alone. The handle kind lives in the declared SLOT TYPE (`Id h a`), not
-- the value; routing it to `resolve` needs either a slot-type dispatch channel
-- (an inference change) or a value-shape probe in the oracle-protected
-- `typeConstructorOf` hot path — neither a clean single-commit flip. The class
-- therefore STAYS inert past 8c Step 1; the flip is its own follow-up (8c-2).
-- See the roadmap's Phase-8c completion notes for the finding + recommendation.
--
-- GAP #1 — dependent `a` (ATTEMPTED in 8b, CARRIED to 8c per the brief's
-- documented fallback — flagged loudly here):
-- the north-star signature is `resolve : h -> Maybe a`, where the referent type
-- `a` threads from the `Id h a` phantom, NOT a free type var. 8b lands the
-- type-level erase (`idReferentType` in `type-foundation.ts`: an `Id h a` TYPE →
-- its referent kind) but does NOT tighten THIS `.fenel` return, because the
-- value-level `$el`/`schemaOf` precedent does not transfer:
--   • `tryCreateInstance`'s `value: $el {call: ["schemaOf", {var: "typeId"}]}`
--     works because `typeId` is a VALUE that names a TYPE — `schemaOf` resolves it
--     directly. `resolve`'s input `h` is a handle naming an INSTANCE; its `{$ref}`
--     VALUE carries only the id — the referent name `a` lives in the type-level
--     `Id h a` phantom, NOT in the runtime value. So no value-level `$el` over the
--     handle can recover `a`.
--   • Recovering `a` from a handle needs instance→type resolution (read the
--     instance, read its `entity_type`, fetch that schema) — the resolution-as-
--     dispatch machinery. 8c Step 1 built ONE half of that (the by-name read
--     `state:getInstanceByName` now exists), but the deref FLIP is deferred (the
--     dispatch-key blocker above), so the value→type recovery still isn't on a
--     live path. `idReferentType` resolves `a` from the TYPE, where the phantom
--     is present; the dependent `.fenel` return resolves `a` from the VALUE,
--     which only the (still-deferred) live flip can do.
-- For now the class keeps the CONCRETE return shape the instances actually
-- produce — a registry instance record, `Maybe<{ }>` (byte-for-byte what
-- `state:getInstance`/`state:getInstanceById`/`getInstanceByName` return). The
-- `{ }` is the documented stopgap, NOT a permanent empty-record return; the flip
-- (8c-2) tightens it to the dependent referent schema when resolution goes live.
-- (`h` is the class param, so it IS in scope; only `a` is deferred — never
-- parametrised as a free var.)
--
-- GAP #2 — the constraint is the `requires`/`$el` "∃ a resolvable instance"
-- obligation, never `=>` sugar (Fenel has none). 8a declares the class only; the
-- obligation surfaces when 8c wires `deref` to it.
--
-- The two instances (UUID / CodeableName) are authored as TS `ClassInstance`
-- consts in `builtin-classes.ts` — the established home for arrow/reference-
-- method instances (boolTruthy / placeResolvable / profileFilterable all live
-- there; `.fenel` arrow-method instances are still deferred, and the `.fenel`
-- loader stores a reference-method value as `{var:…}`, not the dispatch-ready
-- `{$ref}` shape the flip needs). Both resolve targets (getInstanceById /
-- getInstanceByName) are registered functions as of 8c Step 1.
Identifiable h = Identifiable resolve: h -> Maybe<{ }>

-- ============================================================================
-- representation-substrate Phase 1 (2026-06-25) — the `Reprable` class +
-- the `JsRuntime` target marker.
--
-- `Reprable target a` is the pure↔native REPRESENTATION boundary for type `a`
-- under representation target `target`: `lift` crosses pure-ADT → native rep
-- (the PERF direction), `lower` crosses native rep → pure ADT (the REASONING
-- direction), and `native` names the runtime encoding type. This is the
-- Fenel-authored SOURCE OF TRUTH for what was a per-type `repr` string + a
-- hardcoded coercion arm in TS (json-logic.ts) — representation is a target
-- concern, not a type field ([[feedback_no_language_concept_in_ts]]).
--
-- Two params resolve DIFFERENTLY (the design's R1, the `Iso s a` 2-param
-- precedent above):
--   • `a`      — the pure ADT, VALUE-dispatched: it heads `lift`'s arg 0
--                (`a -> native`, a bare class var like `Monoid.mappend`), so
--                `dispatchPositionForSeed` derives arg-0 with NO TS class-seed
--                literal (unlike codec.ts's `iso`/`encodable` hand-written
--                seeds — Reprable's seed is DERIVED in `pureClasses`/`lawClasses`,
--                system-entities.ts).
--   • `target` — the representation target, SCOPE-resolved: it heads no argument
--                position (only `native`, a return-ish `FenelType`, conceptually
--                depends on it), so it cannot come from a value — "choosing a
--                target IS choosing a dispatch scope" (the runtime scope provides
--                `Reprable JsRuntime _`; the Phase-5 storage scope `Reprable
--                Jsonb _`). For Phase 1 only the one runtime target exists.
--
-- The round-trip laws bind `lift`/`lower` (the SEMANTIC definition) to the
-- ledgered native PERF BRANCH (the numeric-tower D1 pattern — pure body =
-- semantics, native = performance, a round-trip law keeps them honest;
-- [[feedback_opaque_is_optimization]]):
--   lower (lift x) ≡ x   (on `a`)        — reprLowerLiftRoundtrip
--   lift (lower n) ≡ n   (on `native`)   — reprLiftLowerRoundtrip
--
-- LAW-FIELD SHAPE — `Verified Boolean`, the `Iso s a` template VERBATIM (this
-- file's codec-class convention). The numeric-tower D7/D8 precedent is the `$el`
-- predicate-field form, which IS the canonical aspiration — but NO class anywhere
-- yet carries an `$el` law field (the tower's `$el` laws have no class/type home
-- until its own Phase-5 class minting; see nat-bridge.fenel:48-50), whereas
-- `Verified Boolean` is the proven, dispatcher-+-catalog-accepted shape every
-- law-bearing class here uses (Iso / Lens / Migration / DecidableMembership /
-- Compilable / Evaluator). So Reprable adopts `Verified Boolean` for catalog
-- parity; the `$el` reconciliation happens fleet-wide when the tower mints its
-- law-bearing rung classes, not as a one-off here. Per the Iso instances
-- (codec/instances.fenel — isoBase64 omits both law fields), Reprable INSTANCES
-- need not populate the law fields; the round-trip is sample-discharged in the
-- oracle (bool-opaque.test.ts), the nat-peano.test.ts D7/D8 precedent.
--
-- `native` is declared `FenelType` (the type-of-types method-field convention
-- Aggregable's `inputType`/`outputType` + AddressFormat's `addressType` use, NOT
-- the raw `{$type:true}` universe constructor `Type` the design sketch wrote —
-- `FenelType` is what the loader's `typeValuedFields` option lowers to a type
-- reference). The instance's `native` lowers via `typeValuedFields: {Reprable:
-- ["native"]}` (system-entities.ts) to `{$named:…}`, not a runtime value.
--
-- R9 — `Reprable` is DISTINCT from the UI `Representational` family
-- (design/representations.md, Iconable/Labelable/… card overrides):
-- `Representational` = how a value is SHOWN; `Reprable` = how a value is ENCODED.
-- ============================================================================

-- The JS-runtime representation target — a nullary marker the dispatch scope
-- keys on (the smallest registered type that names the runtime target; the
-- Phase-5 `Jsonb` storage target slots in as a second nullary marker with no
-- rework). The one genuinely-new type Phase 1 introduces.
JsRuntime = JsRuntime

-- The storage/wire (JSONB) representation target — the SECOND nullary marker,
-- added with no rework exactly as Phase 1 foresaw (representation-substrate
-- Phase 5c). `Reprable Jsonb <type>` is the home for "how a `<type>` value
-- encodes AT REST", the same way `Reprable JsRuntime <type>` is the home for its
-- in-memory rep. Per R4 (per-(target,type) granularity) these are SEPARATE
-- instances from the JsRuntime ones even though the storage native coincides with
-- the runtime native for today's 7 atoms (Step-0 (c)) — a future `bytea`/columnar
-- storage target could diverge, and the marker keeps that door open. The state.ts
-- DB IO leaf (the `stripTopLevelTag` → `store` value path) is this target's
-- ledgered PERF BRANCH (the values already arrive native ⇒ store-as-is IS the
-- byte-compat-verified `lift` fast path; Step-0 (a)/(b) found existing rows
-- uniformly native, so NO data migration — design/representation-as-instance.md
-- R7). Storage *location* is the storage-as-effect workstream's lane (the
-- `HasStateIO`/`StorageHandler` dispatch); storage *encoding* is THIS marker's
-- `Reprable Jsonb` — R7 unifies them conceptually with no collision.
Jsonb = Jsonb

Reprable target a = Reprable native: FenelType lift: a -> native lower: native -> a reprLowerLiftRoundtrip: Verified Boolean reprLiftLowerRoundtrip: Verified Boolean

-- ============================================================================
-- numeric-tower Phase 5 (2026-06-26) — the numeric class trinity `Eq` / `Ord` /
-- `Numeric` (D5). THE TOWER CAPSTONE: with all four rungs operational
-- (Nat/Int/Rational/Real, Phases 1–4) these three classes are what make the
-- tower stop being "four standalone rung body-sets" and become one DISPATCHED
-- numeric type — `add`/`lt`/`eq` over a value select that value's rung instance
-- by reflection (design/unified-dispatch.md), exactly as `mappend`/`fmap` do.
--
-- THE `Monoid`-SHAPE (algebra.fenel) ⇒ DISPATCH-LIVE WITH NO TS SEED. Each
-- method's class var `a` heads a BARE arg-0 (`eq : a -> a -> …`, `add : a -> a ->
-- a`), so `dispatchPositionForSeed` derives arg-0 dispatch from the `.fenel`
-- record with NO hand-written `SpecRecordSeed` (the Monoid / Reprable precedent;
-- the seed is DERIVED in `pureClasses`/`superClasses`, system-entities.ts —
-- `Eq`/`Numeric` are pure-signature, `Ord` carries the `Eq` super so it derives
-- in `superClasses` AFTER `Eq`). [[feedback_typeclasses_are_types]]
-- [[feedback_opaque_is_optimization]]
--
-- (FORK 1 — the partial-ℝ reconciliation, the genuine Rom-call, RESOLVED (B)
-- UNIFORM `Maybe Bool`, the brief's lean.) `Eq a`/`Ord a` return `Maybe Bool`,
-- NOT a total `Bool`. The decidable rungs (Nat/Int/Rational) ALWAYS return `Just
-- …` (a total comparison IS "always-decidable" — the `Just`-wrap is cheap +
-- honest, authored in numeric-compare.fenel); the ℝ rung returns `Nothing` when
-- undecided-within-budget (realEq/realLt's `Maybe Bool`, D4 — equality of Cauchy
-- reals is only semi-decidable). One class hosts both ⇒ ONE `Eq`/`Ord` for the
-- cross-rung `Number` dispatch (no join across two class pairs, the (C) cost).
-- The ℝ FUEL is NOT a class-method arg (which would diverge every decidable rung's
-- arity) — it is closed over by the ℝ instance's default-budget wrappers
-- (realEqDefault/realLtDefault, numeric-compare.fenel); explicit-fuel callers use
-- `realEq`/`realLt` directly (the existing `*Within` pair). NO `HasRealBudget`
-- effect was minted (fence §3 — the lean (B) avoids the effect-keyword
-- anti-pattern). Alternatives (A) associated-result-type + (C) split classes are
-- recorded in the roadmap; this is Rom's ratification call.
--
-- LAWS — STUBS this phase (fence §3). The algebraic laws (add associativity /
-- commutativity, lt transitivity) + the `Ord⇒Eq` consistency are NOT authored as
-- `Verified`/`$el` law fields yet (no kernel proofs — the Phase-4 `converges`
-- precedent). The `Ord⇒Eq` SUPER (the `ordEq: Eq a` field below) IS authored — it
-- is the `requires`/`$el` "∃ a resolvable `Eq` instance" obligation
-- ([[feedback_laws_supers_are_el_dependent_typing]]), the Groupable
-- `referenceable: Referenceable a` super shape — NOT a supers array, NOT GHC
-- dictionary fields. The classes are otherwise law-FREE (Describable/HasDefault
-- precedent) ⇒ no audit spec-law count change; the `$el` algebraic-law fields land
-- with the kernel-proof follow-up.

-- Eq a — decidable-or-semidecidable equality. `eq`/`neq : a -> a -> Maybe Bool`
-- (FORK-1 (B)). The decidable rungs return `Just (…)`; the ℝ rung returns
-- `Nothing` when equality is undecided within budget. D6: this is ALSO the home
-- of the formerly-`String`-typed native `eq`/`neq` — the polymorphic `Eq.eq` the
-- `logic-native.fenel` decls now point at (behaviour unchanged; the String `Eq`
-- IS the lexical compare).
Eq a = Eq eq: a -> a -> Maybe Bool neq: a -> a -> Maybe Bool

-- Ord a — total-or-partial order. Supers Eq (the `ordEq: Eq a` obligation field,
-- erased from the dispatch method set by `seedFromType`'s super classifier, the
-- Groupable/HasHTTP precedent). `lt`/`lte`/`gt`/`gte : a -> a -> Maybe Bool`
-- (FORK-1 (B)). Derived methods (lte/gt/gte) compose lt+eq in the per-rung
-- wrappers (numeric-compare.fenel); a future subsumption may collapse Ord to the
-- minimal-complete `lt` with lte/gt/gte as std fns over Ord (recorded, not done).
Ord a = Ord ordEq: Eq a lt: a -> a -> Maybe Bool lte: a -> a -> Maybe Bool gt: a -> a -> Maybe Bool gte: a -> a -> Maybe Bool

-- Numeric a — the ring operations. add/sub/mul/negate, all TOTAL (`a -> a -> a` /
-- `a -> a`). Instances for the RINGS only — Int / Rational / Real (Nat is NOT a
-- ring: monus is truncated and there is no negate, so `Numeric Nat` is omitted as
-- a mathematically-honest gap; Nat carries Eq/Ord only). The method values
-- reference the Phase 1–4 bodies (intAdd…realNegate) via the function-reference
-- form.
Numeric a = Numeric add: a -> a -> a sub: a -> a -> a mul: a -> a -> a negate: a -> a

-- ============================================================================
-- solver-extension Phase 1 (2026-06-26) — `Enumerable a` (the typed widening
-- the shipped solver fences on). `enumerate.fenel`'s `enumerateSet` returns
-- Nothing for `WsInterval` BY CONSTRUCTION — "the type-erased fence: markers
-- carry no base type, so integer-stepping is unjustifiable (the P5 'NO
-- integer-stepping invented here' rule)." numeric-tower (COMPLETE) supplies the
-- typed `Int` base; this class is the documented widening: a per-base-type
-- successor enumeration. With an `Enumerable Int` instance live, the de-fence
-- (`engine:enumerableSet`, a SIBLING of `enumerateSet`) recovers the base type
-- from the SetExpr's TYPE CONTEXT (fork (A) — PROVIDED, never invented) and
-- steps an integer-typed `WsInterval` into a real finite witness set.
-- [[feedback_typeclasses_are_types]] — it IS an entity type; no separate
-- substrate. [[project_numeric_tower]]
--
-- THE MINIMAL METHOD SET. `enumFromTo : a -> a -> List a` — the bounded range
-- the interval-enumeration arm consumes (`[lo..hi]`). `succ`/`pred` are NOT
-- declared: they ride a later phase when a consumer forces them (the
-- numeric-tower "Numeric Nat omitted until a ring consumer" discipline — author
-- only the method the de-fence actually spends). `Monoid`-shape (the class var
-- `a` heads a BARE arg-0: `enumFromTo : a -> …`) ⇒ it derives arg-0 dispatch in
-- `system-entities.ts` `pureClasses` with NO hand-written TS seed (the Eq /
-- Numeric / Reprable precedent). LAW-FREE (no `Verified` field) ⇒ no audit
-- spec-law count change; the "enumFromTo lo hi enumerates EXACTLY [lo..hi]
-- inclusive, in order" law is a deferred `$el`/`Verified` field (the Eq/Ord/
-- Numeric algebraic-law precedent).
Enumerable a = Enumerable enumFromTo: a -> a -> List a
